In this lesson we'll look at how to turn a pattern you've spotted into a formula that works for every case, not just the ones you tried. The habit is simple. First, work out a few specific values. Then look for what stays the same, like a constant difference or a constant ratio. Once you see the pattern, swap the actual numbers for letters and write a single rule. Two patterns come up a lot. A constant difference gives a straight line rule, where y is a constant times x plus another constant. A constant ratio gives an exponential rule, which you'll meet later on. The big payoff is that a general formula lets you predict the hundredth term, or even the minus seventh term, without working through everything in between. A quick heads-up though: two or three examples aren't always enough. The numbers one, two, four, eight look like they grow evenly, but the real rule is exponential, two raised to the power of n minus one. So always double-check, maybe by plotting your points against your formula. Now let's see it in action.